cbs news senior business analyst jill  schlesinger joins us now for more about  the surging rate of inflation and the  rest of the nation's economic picture  jill good morning to you first question  you know where did we see the biggest  rise when you look at inflation as a  whole  well let's start with the places that we  encounter most frequently look at energy  energy costs up over 33 percent from a  year ago and of that energy component  gas prices are just soaring up nearly 60  percent from a year ago that's the  largest annual increase since the spring  of 1980.  then you go on to the grocery store  you're driving to the grocery store and  as you saw in that piece everything  costing more food costs up 6.1 percent  and again it's those proteins that are  really driving a lot of the rise in food  costs those costs are not going away  anytime soon one little glimmer of good  news was that services which usually  comprise about 60 percent of consumer  spending those costs are rising but not  nearly as quickly as all those commodity  prices  joe you mentioned those gas prices and  the high and looking back to 1980 how is  this different though than that raising  inflation that we saw in the late 70s  and early 80s  well it's very different because in that  time we had very slow economic growth  and right now we've got robust economic  growth part of the reason why this is  such an interesting time is that you  know we've been sort of a little bit uh  on pins and needles waiting for things  to actually get open and grow now  they're open and people are going out  and going crazy so the reality is that  right now this inflation it's not going  away anytime soon but it is important to  know the companies especially the  profitable ones they are starting to pay  their employees more so that should help  joe let me ask you about that timeline  the president says he believes inflation  has peaked do you believe that  well i don't think it has peaked yet and  i think that this is a econ 101 lesson  in spades when you have constrained  supply we do have that a lot of supply  shortages and surging demand we have  been waiting for almost two years to  spend some of the more than two trillion  dollars that we have amassed as  americans that combination is explosive  it's why we're seeing such high levels  of inflation it's likely to recede over  the next year but you know what we could  reset at a higher inflation rate than we  were before the pandemic before the  pandemic 2019 1.8 percent was the cpi  what we're probably looking at a year  from now two and a half maybe three  percent and that's something americans  are going to have to get used to  next week jill the federal reserve will  hold its last meeting of the year what  should we expect and why should people  be paying attention to this  well the federal reserve led by jerome  powell is going to change its monetary  policy and this is very important before  his congressional testimony jerome  powell had  originally said oh inflation is  transitory it's temporary now they've  shifted during that congressional  testimony powell said that the fed is  going to stop buying all the bonds it  was doing as an emergency measure and  that will pave the way for an increase  in short-term interest rates all of this  could happen sooner than previously  expected the fed will lay out its plans  next week and listen gang i know you  sort of have your eyes glazed over when  we talk about the federal reserve but  this impacts everything from your  savings rates to your credit card  interest charges to maybe being able to  borrow money for a small business so i  promise i'll cover it for you so you  don't have to pay attention but it's  really important  you are always covering it jill and for  that we do appreciate it thank you
 

